    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 142252\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n252 PM PST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Calm and dry conditions for the rest of the weekend, cooling trend \n  next week\n\n* Additional precipitation and cooler weather arrives Monday and \n  Tuesday and again in the second half of next week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through Friday\n\nMuch calmer and drier conditions have settled in over the region \ntoday as brief ridging sets up for the next 24-48 hours. Satellite \nimagery this afternoon shows lingering moisture and clouds over \nMohave County AZ and points east while the rest of the Mohave desert \nand lower Great Basin have cleared out. Current LAS ACARS soundings \nreveal a much drier airmass in place with a strong subsidence \ninversion at 700mb and a deep mixed layer below with relatively calm \nwinds. While winds are much lower than they were yesterday, there \nare still some pockets of terrain enhanced W-NW winds across \nportions of North and Western Clark County and Mohave County. But \noverall a dry and benign weekend with highs in the 60s to low 70s \nfor desert valleys. Similar conditions expected for the latter half \nof the weekend and into Monday; however expect clouds to be on the \nincrease as upper level moisture advects in as the ridge axis pushes \neast. Cloud cover thickens on Monday resulting in temperatures \ndropping by a few degrees. Guidance today did come in a little drier \nwith the next system, raising confidence that much of the day Monday \ncould remain dry as well despite increased clouds. \n\nBy Monday evening, deep troughing pushes inland along the California \nCoast with a 90 kt SSW jet out ahead of it. While there is good low \nand mid level southerly flow ahead of the trough advecting moisture \nnorthward, guidance today has come down on both PW and QPF, \nindicating slightly weaker moisture advection into the Mohave \nDesert. ECMWF ensemble mean PW is around 0.80\" for Las Vegas; \nwhereas guidance was showing upwards of 1.00\" yesterday. Models \ntoday are also coming in a few hours slowly with the system, \nbringing precipitation into the Eastern Sierra Monday evening, \nspreading eastward into Nevada late Monday night. Greatest coverage \nof rainfall and showers looks to be across Nye and Lincoln Counties \nand higher terrain Monday night with more scattered showers likely \nover Clark County. Tuesday morning, precipitation arrives in Mohave \nCounty while a drying trend begins in Inyo and San Bernardino \nCounties as the trough axis pushes through. Rainfall totals from \nMonday evening into Tuesday look modest (0.01 to 0.25\") for all but \nNortheast Clark Co, Mohave Co, and Lincoln Co which will range from \n0.30 to 0.5\" after showers exit to the east. Currently, snow in the \nupper elevations of the Sierra from this next system looks to stay \nrelatively light as the best forcing and moisture advection remain \nsouth and east. Additional chances for rain expected later in the \nweek as another system will likely be on the heals of the departing \none. \n\nYet another trough approaches the West Coast Thursday moving inland \nby Friday bringing another round of light showers and gusty winds to \nthe region. With continued systems impacting the region and an \noverall troughing pattern persisting, expect a cooling trend to \ncontinue for most of the week with highs near to below average. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Light\nnortherly to northwesterly winds continue overnight and into \ntomorrow morning. By late morning, winds shift back to the \neast/northeast, with speeds of less than 10 knots. High cloud \ncover will increase through tomorrow. SCT clouds with bases as low\nas 8kft cannot be ruled out in tomorrow evening. \n\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Light winds with speeds \nunder 10 knots are expected at area terminals through Sunday \nafternoon for most locations. Southerly winds with sustained speeds \nover 10 knots and gusts in excess of 20 knots develop late tomorrow \nmorning in the Owens Valley including KBIH. Mid and high cloud cover \nwill increase throughout Sunday from the northwest to the\nsoutheast.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Moore\nAVIATION...Meltzer\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
